{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Task Commit",
  "description": "When a task is completed, this hook analyzes the changes and prepares a detailed commit message for you to use.",
  "version": "4",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileEdited",
    "patterns": [
      "**/.kiro/specs/**/tasks.md"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "You are an automated task completion assistant and a strict editor. The `tasks.md` file that triggered this hook was just edited.\n\n1. **Read Style Guide:** First, you must read and internalize the rules from the style guide located in the same directory as this hook: `jeremy_fuksa_technical_writing_style_guide_v1.1.0.md`.\n\n2. **Verify Completion:** Scan the file to confirm that a task was marked as complete (a line changed from `[ ]` to `[x]`). If no task was completed, stop immediately.\n\n3. **Identify Task & Changes:** If a task was completed, extract the full text of that task. Then, run `git diff HEAD~1` to analyze all recent code changes related to this task.\n\n4. **Generate Commit Message:** Based on your analysis, create a detailed, multi-line commit message that follows both the Conventional Commits specification and the project's writing style guide.\n   - **Parse Type and Subject:** Look for a type prefix (e.g., `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`) at the beginning of the completed task's text. If a prefix is found, use it. If not, default to `feat`. The subject line must be `type(tasks): [task text]`.\n   - **Compose Body:** The body of the commit message should be a bulleted list summarizing the key implementation details and code changes you discovered in the git diff.\n   - **Enforce Style:** Ensure the entire message (subject and body) strictly adheres to the style guide you read in step 1.\n\n5. **Prepare Commit Command:** Finally, create the full command needed to commit the work. The command should stage the `tasks.md` file that was changed and use the generated commit message. Output the complete, ready-to-paste command to the console. Do not execute the commit yourself."
  }
}
